The United Nations Framework Convention on Climate Change (UNFCCC)
The United Nations Framework Convention on Climate Change (UNFCCC) was established in
1992 at the Rio Earth Summit. It became the first global framework for addressing climate change,
recognising it as a shared problem that requires coordinated action. With 198 parties, the UNFCCC
has near-universal membership, demonstrating a broad commitment to addressing climate issues.
The Role of the UNFCCC
● The UNFCCC serves as the primary international forum for climate action, setting the
framework for how countries address climate change through regular conferences, known
as Conferences of the Parties (COPs).
○ At these conferences, countries assess progress, negotiate binding and non-binding
agreements, and establish financial and technological support for developing
nations.
● The UNFCCC does not mandate specific emission reduction targets; instead, it encourages
countries to create their own goals and report their emissions, providing a flexible structure
that allows for broad participation.
● Since the 1990s, the UNFCCC has established major protocols and agreements, including
the Kyoto Protocol (1997) and the Paris Agreement (2015), which have set increasingly
ambitious targets for climate action.
The Impact and Significance of the UNFCCC
● Through regular COP meetings, the UNFCCC has institutionalised climate discussions,
ensuring that climate change remains a top priority on the international agenda. COPs have
become crucial events where countries come together to negotiate agreements and assess
collective progress.
● The UNFCCC has facilitated the signing of key international agreements designed to
tackle climate change, including the Kyoto Protocol and Paris Climate Agreement.
● The UNFCCC has created mechanisms such as the Green Climate Fund (GCF) to
support developing countries in both mitigating and adapting to climate change.
○ The GCF, established in 2010, aimed to provide $100 billion annually by 2020 from
developed to developing nations.
● The UNFCCC has established systems for countries to report their emissions and
climate actions, fostering greater transparency. Regular reporting helps hold countries
accountable and enables the global community to monitor progress.
○ For example, Japan received criticism after scaling back its emissions targets in the
wake of the Fukushima nuclear disaster in 2011, as it increased reliance on fossil
fuels. This shift led to calls from the UN and environmental groups for Japan to
revisit its climate strategy.
The Limitations of the UNFCCC
Despite its achievements, the UNFCCC has faced challenges in implementing effective global
climate policies, primarily due to its reliance on voluntary commitments by national governments
and consensus-driven agreements:
● The UNFCCC lacks the power to enforce emissions reductions, relying instead on
voluntary commitments. This has led to uneven compliance, as some countries fail to meet
their targets without facing direct consequences.
● The UNFCCC’s approach to climate responsibility has often been challenged by divisions
between developed and developing nations.
https://www.politicsexplained.co.uk/ 6
○ Developing countries argue that developed nations, being historically responsible
for most emissions, should bear greater responsibility for reducing emissions and
providing financial support.
● Although the Green Climate Fund was intended to support developing countries,
contributions have fallen short of the $100 billion annual target.
○ In 2019, the Organisation for Economic Co-operation and Development (OECD)
reported that only $79.6 billion was mobilised, leaving a significant gap of over $20
billion.
○ This funding gap has hindered the ability of developing nations to implement
necessary adaptation and mitigation projects.
International Agreements to Tackle Climate Change
Over the past four decades, a number of international agreements have been signed to address
the escalating threat of climate change. These agreements aim to set binding or aspirational
targets for emissions reductions, promote sustainable development, and foster international
cooperation. Each agreement reflects evolving approaches to climate governance, from early
frameworks to more comprehensive recent commitments.
The Rio Earth Summit (1992)
The Rio Earth Summit, officially known as the United Nations Conference on Environment and
Development (UNCED), took place in Rio de Janeiro in 1992. This landmark summit brought
together representatives from 178 countries to address environmental challenges, including
climate change, biodiversity loss, and deforestation. The Rio Summit marked the first major
international commitment to sustainable development and established foundational frameworks
that have shaped climate policy ever since.
Key Measures:
● Agenda 21: A comprehensive, non-binding action plan aimed at promoting sustainable
development globally, covering areas such as poverty reduction, waste management, and
conservation.
https://www.politicsexplained.co.uk/ 7
● The UN Framework Conventions: The Rio Summit led to the creation of two critical
frameworks—the United Nations Framework Convention on Climate Change (UNFCCC)
and the Convention on Biological Diversity (CBD)—to coordinate global responses to
climate and biodiversity challenges.
● The Principle of “Common but Differentiated Responsibilities” (CBDR): Recognising
that developed nations were historically responsible for the majority of greenhouse gas
emissions, the CBDR principle established that developed countries should lead in
addressing climate change and support developing nations’ efforts.
Extent of Follow-Through:
While the Rio Summit was instrumental in setting up frameworks for climate action, its measures
were largely aspirational, and actual implementation has been mixed. Agenda 21, for example,
provided a valuable roadmap but lacked binding obligations, leading to inconsistent adoption
among countries. Moreover, while the CBDR principle helped bridge developed and developing
nations’ perspectives, it has also been a source of division, with disagreements over the extent of
developed countries’ financial and technical responsibilities. Overall, the Rio Summit laid essential
groundwork but highlighted the need for more concrete, enforceable commitments.
The Kyoto Protocol (1997)
The Kyoto Protocol was adopted in 1997 as an international treaty under the UNFCCC, marking
the first time countries agreed to binding greenhouse gas (GHG) emissions targets. The Protocol
set legally binding targets for industrialised nations only, reflecting the principle that developed
countries bear primary responsibility for historical emissions. It came into force in 2005 after
achieving ratification by a sufficient number of countries.
Key Measures:
● Binding Emission Targets for Developed Nations: The Kyoto Protocol required 37
industrialised countries, including EU members, Japan, and Canada, to reduce GHG
emissions to 5% below 1990 levels during the first commitment period (2008-2012).
● Emissions Trading and Market Mechanisms: The Protocol established mechanisms like
carbon trading and the Clean Development Mechanism (CDM), allowing countries to earn
credits by investing in emissions-reduction projects in developing countries.
○ This introduced flexibility, enabling countries to meet targets through investment
rather than solely through domestic reductions.
● Second Commitment Period (Doha Amendment): In 2012, the Protocol was extended
with a second commitment period (2013-2020), under the Doha Amendment. However, this
extension saw lower levels of engagement, with major emitters, including the US, Canada,
and Japan, either withdrawing or not taking on new targets.
Extent of Follow-Through:
Despite its ambitious design, the Kyoto Protocol faced significant limitations in practice, largely
from developed states who struggled to meet their emissions targets and resented the fact that
developing economies like China and India were exempt. Several major emitters, including the
United States, declined to ratify the treaty, weakening its impact. Additionally, countries like Canada
withdrew after failing to meet their targets, highlighting the challenges of enforcing binding
commitments without stronger accountability mechanisms.
While some nations/regional organisations, including the EU, Russia and Japan, achieved
reductions beyond their targets, global emissions continued to rise overall. The Protocol’s limited
