The Paris Agreement (2015)
The Paris Agreement, adopted at COP21 in 2015, is one of the most ambitious international
climate agreements to date, with near-universal participation. Unlike the Kyoto Protocol, which
focused solely on developed nations, the Paris Agreement requires all countries to take part in
emissions reduction efforts, making it a landmark in global climate governance. Its main goal is to
limit global warming to well below 2°C above pre-industrial levels, with efforts to limit the increase
to 1.5°C, which the IPCC identified as critical for avoiding the most severe climate impacts.
Key Measures:
The Paris Agreement marked a transformative moment in climate governance by creating an
inclusive, flexible framework that requires climate action from all nations. Its bottom-up structure,
based on NDCs, gives countries the autonomy to set ambitious yet achievable goals tailored to
their circumstances. The emphasis on keeping global warming below 1.5°C represented a critical
shift in urgency and ambition, aligning with scientific warnings of catastrophic risks if warming
exceeds this threshold.
● Nationally Determined Contributions (NDCs): Each country submits its own climate
action plan, known as an NDC, detailing its emissions reduction targets and strategies.
Countries are encouraged to revise and strengthen their NDCs every five years.
● Global Stocktake: The Agreement includes a “Global Stocktake” every five years to
assess collective progress towards meeting the goals of limiting global warming to 1.5°C or
2°C. This mechanism is intended to increase transparency and accountability.
● Climate Finance for Developing Nations: The Paris Agreement reaffirmed the
commitment to mobilise $100 billion per year in climate finance from developed to
developing countries, with a plan to increase financing beyond 2025.
● Support for Adaptation and Loss and Damage: The Agreement recognises the
importance of adaptation measures and the concept of “loss and damage,” acknowledging
that certain impacts of climate change, particularly in vulnerable regions, may be
irreversible. This has led to discussions about compensatory support for affected countries.
Extent of Follow-Through:
● While the Paris Agreement has inspired many countries to set ambitious targets, progress
has been inconsistent. The first Global Stocktake, conducted in 2023, revealed that the
https://www.politicsexplained.co.uk/ 10
world is not on track to meet the 1.5°C target, with current policies projected to result in a
3.1°C temperature rise by the end of the century.
● Major emitters, including the United States and China, have faced challenges in aligning
their policies with the necessary emission reductions.
○ As the world’s largest emitter of greenhouse gases, China pledged to peak its
carbon dioxide emissions before 2030 and achieve carbon neutrality by 2060.
However, the country’s continued reliance on coal-fired power plants and rapid
industrial growth have led to rising emissions, making it challenging to align with its
Paris Agreement commitments.
■ While China has made significant investments in renewable energy, the pace
of transition has been insufficient to offset the emissions from its expanding
industrial sector.
○ Despite rejoining the Paris Agreement in 2021 and setting ambitious targets to
reduce greenhouse gas emissions by 50-52% below 2005 levels by 2030, the U.S.
has encountered obstacles in implementing effective climate policies.
■ The growing energy demands of artificial intelligence (AI) technologies have
significantly disrupted supply, casting doubt on the U.S.’s ability to
decarbonise its electricity system by 2035.
■ Additionally, Republican opposition and Trump’s rejection of the importance
of climate change have prevented comprehensive climate legislation being
passed, delaying progress toward emission reduction goals.
● Additionally, the $100 billion annual finance goal for developing countries was not met until
2022, two years later than the original 2020 target, highlighting ongoing challenges in
mobilising climate finance.
○ Nevertheless, the Paris Agreement has been instrumental in embedding climate
action into national policies worldwide and remains the central framework for
coordinating international climate efforts.
The Strengths and Weaknesses of International Agreements
Arguments That International Agreements Are Effective
They Have Facilitated Global Commitment to Tackling Climate Change
International climate agreements have been essential in building global consensus and formalising
commitment to climate action.
● The Paris Agreement, signed in 2015, brought together 194 countries, including major
emitters like the United States, China, and India, to pledge to limit global warming to well
below 2°C, with efforts to stay within 1.5°C.
○ This consensus is particularly significant because it includes developing nations and
the world’s largest emitters, who had been historically reluctant to commit to legally
binding reductions.
○ Through the Paris Agreement, nations established a shared goal to reduce
greenhouse gas emissions in ways that suit their economic and environmental
contexts, reinforcing the principle of “common but differentiated responsibilities” that
was first set out in the Rio Earth Summit.
● The Paris Agreement has also provided a platform for vulnerable nations, such as the
Marshall Islands, Fiji, and other Pacific Island nations, to advocate for the 1.5°C target,
which is crucial for their survival in the face of rising sea levels.
https://www.politicsexplained.co.uk/ 11
● Additionally, the 2021 COP26 conference in Glasgow saw 190 countries agreeing to phase
down coal and 130 countries pledging to halt deforestation by 2030—both landmark
commitments that were facilitated by the Paris Agreement and its supporting framework.
○ The Agreement has thus set the foundation for continued global cooperation,
allowing countries to join collective actions that may have been politically or
economically challenging to achieve unilaterally.
They Have Influenced National Policies and Held Countries Accountable
● International climate agreements have influenced domestic climate policies and embedded
global goals into national legislative frameworks. In particular, the Paris Agreement has
done so as it requires countries to submit and update Nationally Determined Contributions
(NDCs) every five years, pushing them to develop and refine ambitious climate strategies.
○ The Agreement’s reporting and accountability measures were tested during the first
Global Stocktake in 2023, where countries were held accountable for progress
toward their NDCs. This accountability framework encourages countries to take
corrective measures, as the public and international community can now monitor
progress in a transparent, structured manner.
● In the UK, the Climate Change Act of 2008—later updated to include a net-zero target by
2050—was strongly influenced by the Kyoto Protocol and subsequent agreements, setting
a legal requirement for carbon budgeting and emissions reductions.
○ This act made the UK the first country in the world to adopt legally binding
emissions targets, inspiring similar legislation in countries like Sweden and New
Zealand, which followed suit with their own net-zero commitments by 2050.
● The European Union’s European Green Deal is another example of how the Paris
Agreement has driven ambitious policy reforms. The Green Deal commits EU member
states to achieve net-zero emissions by 2050, with intermediate targets to cut emissions by
at least 55% by 2030.
○ To achieve this, the EU has introduced a series of initiatives, including the European
Climate Law, which legally binds member states to these targets, and the Carbon
Border Adjustment Mechanism, which places a carbon tariff on imports.
● Beyond Europe, countries like South Korea and Japan have also set net-zero goals by
2050, influenced by the Paris Agreement.
They Have Addressed the Need for Developed States to Help Developing States
● International climate agreements have placed a strong emphasis on the historical
responsibility of developed nations to support developing countries in mitigating and
adapting to climate impacts.
● The Paris Agreement reaffirmed the commitment to mobilise $100 billion annually from
developed countries to assist developing nations.
○ This funding commitment has been channeled through mechanisms like the Green
Climate Fund (GCF), established to support climate resilience and sustainable
development projects in vulnerable regions.
○ While the $100 billion target has been inconsistently met, the GCF has nonetheless
financed impactful projects across Africa, Asia, and Latin America. Further, it was
met in 2022, when developed countries provided and mobilised a total of $115.9 billion
in climate finance for developing countries and though comprehensive data hasn’t yet
been released, it seems likely it will be met in 2023 too.
● In Morocco, the GCF funded a $98 million project to develop the world’s largest
concentrated solar power plant, the Noor Ouarzazate complex, which has significantly
boosted the country’s renewable energy capacity and reduced its reliance on fossil fuels.
https://www.politicsexplained.co.uk/ 12
○ In Bangladesh, the GCF has invested $40 million in a project to build
cyclone-resilient infrastructure and improve early warning systems for communities
at risk from extreme weather events.
● These programs are essential for empowering developing nations to create locally relevant
climate solutions. While challenges remain, the commitment to supporting developing
states has fostered a more inclusive approach to climate action, acknowledging the
unequal capacity of countries to address climate change and working to bridge this gap.
Arguments That International Agreements Are Not Effective
International Agreements Have Lacked Enforcement Mechanisms and Haven’t Been
Ambitious Enough:
● A significant limitation of international climate agreements, including the Paris Agreement,
is the absence of strong enforcement mechanisms. While agreements encourage countries
to set emissions reduction targets, there are no binding consequences for non-compliance,
allowing countries to miss their targets without facing sanctions or penalties.
○ This lack of accountability is evident in the Paris Agreement, where countries are
only required to submit Nationally Determined Contributions (NDCs) every five
years but are not legally bound to meet them.
○ The Kyoto Protocol, while containing legally binding targets, suffered from a lack of
engagement, as major emitters like the United States refused to ratify it, weakening
its impact.
● This lack of enforcement has contributed to a global climate response that is insufficiently
ambitious; current NDCs place the world on a trajectory for a 2.7°C to 3.1°C rise in
temperatures by the end of the century, far above the Paris Agreement’s 1.5°C target.
● Additionally, the voluntary nature of these targets has allowed countries to set insufficiently
ambitious goals. For example, under its initial NDC, Brazil committed to reducing emissions
by 37% by 2025 but has since weakened enforcement of environmental protections,
leading to increased deforestation in the Amazon.
● The global climate response has thus been hampered by a structure that relies on goodwill
rather than enforceable commitments.
○ Without stronger mechanisms for holding countries accountable, international
agreements remain largely aspirational and fall short of driving the necessary levels
of ambition to prevent catastrophic climate outcomes.
Many Countries Have Failed to Meet Their Targets
● Despite signing international agreements, numerous countries have failed to meet their
climate targets, demonstrating the limitations of voluntary compliance frameworks.
○ For instance, under the Copenhagen Accord, Canada pledged to cut its greenhouse
gas emissions by 17% below 2005 levels by 2020. However, due to increased oil
and gas production, Canada’s emissions actually rose by 21% above 1990 levels,
causing the country to withdraw from the Kyoto Protocol in 2011 and face criticism
for failing to achieve its targets.
○ Similarly, Australia committed to reducing emissions by 5% from 2000 levels by
2020, but its reliance on coal and natural gas production resulted in continued high
emissions, making it one of the highest per capita emitters globally.
● Even under the Paris Agreement, where countries have set more ambitious targets, there
are still substantial gaps in meeting those commitments.
https://www.politicsexplained.co.uk/ 13
○ China, the world’s largest emitter, pledged to peak its emissions before 2030 and
reach carbon neutrality by 2060, yet continues to approve new coal-fired power
plants and faces rising emissions due to industrial growth.
○ The United States, despite rejoining the Paris Agreement in 2021 under Biden, has
struggled with inconsistent climate policies due to political shifts, and its emissions
reduction targets have been reversed once more after the reelection of Trump and
his “drill baby, drill” attitude to fossil fuels.
● The failure of these major emitters to align with their stated goals reflects the challenges of
relying on voluntary targets, which can be easily adjusted or ignored without legal
repercussions. As a result, global progress toward the 1.5°C target remains inconsistent
and inadequate.
Developed States Haven’t Supported Developing States as Much as Promised
● International agreements have consistently emphasised the need for developed countries
to support developing nations in climate mitigation and adaptation efforts, yet this support
has fallen short of expectations, as developed states have prioritised their own economic
progress.
● Developed countries pledged to mobilise $100 billion annually in climate finance by 2020 to
assist developing nations, but this target was repeatedly missed.
○ In 2019, the Organisation for Economic Co-operation and Development (OECD)
reported that only $79.6 billion was mobilised, leaving a $20 billion gap. Although
this target was eventually met in 2022, the inconsistency and delays have strained
trust between developed and developing countries.
○ Nations in Sub-Saharan Africa, highly vulnerable to climate impacts, have struggled
to fund necessary adaptation measures, such as building resilient infrastructure and
securing water resources, due to limited financial support.
● Furthermore, even the funds that are allocated often fall short of addressing critical needs.
For example, while the Green Climate Fund (GCF) has provided financing for renewable
energy projects and disaster resilience in countries like Kenya and Bangladesh, the scale of
assistance remains inadequate relative to the challenges faced.
○ In 2021, the Prime Minister of Barbados Mia Mottley called out developed countries
for their limited contributions, arguing that climate finance must go beyond the $100
billion pledge to address both mitigation and adaptation adequately.
