Important Developments to International Efforts to Tackle Climate Change
Over the Past Decade
Trump Withdraws The U.S. From The Paris Agreement Again
In January 2025, Trump withdrew the U.S. from the Paris Climate Agreement for a second time,
arguing that it was unfair to American workers and industries, whilst also reflecting his long-held
scepticism of climate change. Alongside this move, the administration aggressively promoted new
fossil fuel projects, with the Trump administration lifting environmental restrictions on offshore
drilling and opening up federal lands for oil and gas exploration. Trump kept repeating the slogan
“drill, baby, drill”. This is a clear example of the limitations of the Paris Agreement, which is
non-binding and relies entirely on national political will to set and meet emissions targets. Without
legal enforcement major emitters like the U.S. can simply leave whenever they want.
The “Loss and Damage” Framework
The inclusion of “loss and damage” in COP negotiations reflects the growing pressure from
vulnerable nations for compensation for irreversible climate impacts. The establishment of the Loss
and Damage Fund at COP27 was a milestone, recognising that developed nations, historically
responsible for much of the world’s greenhouse gas emissions, have a duty to support countries
suffering the most. The fund provides financial support to countries that are disproportionately
affected by severe climate impacts, such as extreme weather events and rising sea levels. It
recognises that vulnerable nations, often least responsible for greenhouse gas emissions, are
facing irreversible climate losses and require compensation and assistance.
Acceleration of Renewable Energy Investment
Over the past decade, international agreements and national policies have spurred significant
investment in renewable energy. Countries such as China, the United States, and India have
rapidly increased their renewable capacity, with China leading in solar and wind energy installation.
The International Renewable Energy Agency (IRENA) reported a record $500 billion in global
renewable energy investments in 2022 alone. However, this investment remains uneven, as many
developing countries still lack access to affordable renewable energy technology.
The Growing Role of Civil Society and The Private Sector
Civil society, including advocacy groups, youth movements, and private sector stakeholders, has
increasingly influenced climate action, often pushing governments to adopt stronger policies.
Figures like Greta Thunberg have mobilised millions worldwide, placing pressure on leaders to
commit to more ambitious targets. The private sector has also played a larger role, with companies
committing to net-zero emissions and investing in sustainable technologies.
The Efforts of Other Organisations/Groups to Tackle Climate
Change
In addition to international agreements and state-level actions, a range of other organisations and
groups have played significant roles in tackling climate change. Regional organisations, civil
society, city networks, and various institutions have put pressure on national governments and
driven progress in areas where international action has fallen short.
