Regional Organisations
The European Union (EU)
The EU has been one of the most proactive regional bodies in addressing climate change, with its
European Green Deal aiming for net-zero emissions by 2050.
● The EU has implemented binding climate legislation, including the European Climate Law,
which requires member states to collectively reduce greenhouse gas emissions by at least
55% by 2030 compared to 1990 levels.
● Through these efforts, the EU has already met and, in some cases, exceeded earlier
targets, reducing emissions by approximately 30% from 1990 levels by 2020—ahead of the
original goal of 20%.
● This success reflects strong implementation of policies like the EU Emissions Trading
System (ETS), the world’s largest carbon market, which caps emissions for key sectors like
energy and heavy industry.
○ The ETS has played a crucial role in incentivising emissions reductions, cutting
emissions in regulated sectors by nearly 43% between 2005 and 2020.
○ It has also served as a model for similar emissions trading systems worldwide, with
countries like South Korea and regions like California adopting similar programs.
African Union (AU)
● The AU has adopted climate action frameworks that focus on climate adaptation, resilience,
and sustainable development, recognising the vulnerability of many African nations to
climate impacts.
● Programs like the Africa Adaptation Initiative (AAI) and the Africa Renewable Energy
Initiative (AREI) play crucial roles in helping African countries tackle climate challenges that
disproportionately impact the continent.
https://www.politicsexplained.co.uk/ 19
○ The Africa Adaptation Initiative, launched in 2015, focuses on improving resilience
through projects that enhance water security, climate-smart agriculture, and disaster
risk management, aiming to reduce the vulnerability of communities to extreme
weather and food insecurity.
○ The Africa Renewable Energy Initiative seeks to expand renewable energy
generation, with a goal to add at least 300 GW of renewable capacity by 2030.
■ This initiative prioritises energy access for rural and underserved areas,
promoting solar, wind, and hydro projects that reduce reliance on fossil fuels
while supporting economic development.
● The AU has also advocated for increased climate finance from developed nations,
highlighting the need for resources to support African countries facing disproportionate
climate impacts.
Association of Southeast Asian Nations (ASEAN)
● ASEAN has focused especially on disaster risk reduction and the promotion of sustainable
energy throughout the region.
● The ASEAN Climate Change Initiative (ACCI) encourages member states to develop and
implement national climate action plans that align with regional goals, promoting resilience
to climate impacts such as floods, droughts, and coastal erosion.
○ One of ASEAN’s key commitments is to increase renewable energy’s share in the
regional energy mix to 23% by 2025, an ambitious target given the region’s reliance
on fossil fuels.
○ As of recent assessments, ASEAN has made steady progress toward this target,
with several member states, including the Philippines and Vietnam, achieving
significant increases in solar and wind capacity.
● ASEAN has also prioritised joint research and knowledge-sharing to address the unique
climate challenges faced by Southeast Asian countries, including the high risk of sea-level
rise affecting low-lying areas and intensified extreme weather events like typhoons.
○ For example, the ASEAN Centre for Biodiversity facilitates research on ecosystem
resilience, and ASEAN disaster response initiatives have strengthened regional
cooperation for swift disaster response and recovery.
Global Civil Society
Global civil society has played a crucial role in mobilising public awareness and applying pressure
on governments to strengthen their climate commitments. Civil society’s efforts span from
grassroots movements to large-scale funding initiatives.
Youth Climate Movements
● Figures like Greta Thunberg and movements such as Fridays for Future have united
millions of young people worldwide, demanding urgent and ambitious climate policies.
○ Since Thunberg’s first solo climate strike in 2018, Fridays for Future has inspired
over 14 million participants in more than 7,500 cities across 125 countries.
● This movement has leveraged social media and organised public demonstrations,
amplifying youth voices and placing climate change at the center of public debate.
○ In Germany, the movement influenced the government’s decision to set earlier
deadlines for coal phaseout and stricter emissions targets.
○ Similarly, in Sweden, Fridays for Future helped spark debates that led to stronger
commitments in the nation’s Climate Policy Framework, which targets net-zero
emissions by 2045.
