Environmental NGOs
Organisations like Greenpeace, the World Wildlife Fund (WWF), and Friends of the Earth have
long been at the forefront of climate advocacy, using a combination of research, public campaigns,
and litigation to hold corporations and governments accountable.
● Greenpeace has led high-profile campaigns against deforestation in the Amazon,
partnering with local groups to reduce illegal logging and protect indigenous lands.
● In 2019, WWF launched the “New Deal for Nature and People” initiative, urging
governments to adopt ambitious policies that address biodiversity loss alongside climate
change, with a goal to conserve 30% of global land and seas by 2030.
● In 2021, Friends of the Earth, alongside other NGOs, won a landmark case against Royal
Dutch Shell, with a Dutch court ordering the company to cut its CO₂ emissions by 45% by
2030, setting a global precedent for holding corporations legally accountable for their
climate impact.
Foundations and Philanthropists:
Major foundations like the Bill and Melinda Gates Foundation and Bloomberg Philanthropies have
made significant contributions to climate initiatives, especially in areas of clean energy and
resilience. By directing resources to underserved areas, these foundations not only drive
innovation but also promote equity in climate solutions.
● Bloomberg Philanthropies, led by former New York City Mayor Michael Bloomberg, has
committed over $500 million through its Beyond Coal campaign to phase out coal plants in
the United States, resulting in the retirement of over 60% of U.S. coal plants since 2010.
● In India, Bloomberg Philanthropies has supported initiatives to transition urban energy
systems toward renewables, partnering with the government to expand solar installations.
● The Bill and Melinda Gates Foundation has focused on climate adaptation for smallholder
farmers, especially in Africa, funding research on drought-resistant crops and innovative
farming practices to enhance food security under changing climate conditions.
○ In 2022 alone, it donated $1.4 billion to help small farmers adapt to climate change.
Multinational Corporations
ExxonMobil’s Influence On U.S. Foreign Policy - Example Of How Multinational Corporations
Can Undermine Efforts To Tackle Climate Change:
● Under both Trump administrations U.S. oil and gas giant ExxonMobil has played an
important role in influencing U.S. foreign policy to be aligned with its commercial interests.
○ This is including through supporting oil and gas production domestically and through
supporting friendly relations with Russia, as ExxonMobil has major commercial
interests in drilling in the arctic, in partnership with Russian state-owned oil
companies.
○ In the first Trump administration, this included appointing Rex Tillerson as Secretary
of State, who was CEO of ExxonMobil until 2016 and in the second Trump
administration its influence can be seen in the push for a quick peace deal to end
the war in Ukraine, which damaged ExxonMobil’s interests due to resulting the
sanctions on Russia.
● Together, this shows how MNCs - especially oil and gas companies - can negatively impact
the environment by promoting their commercial and economic interests.
