Other Organisations/Groups
In addition to national governments and international agreements, city networks, regional
coalitions, and other institutions have played essential roles in advancing climate action.
C40 Cities Climate Leadership Group
● The C40 Cities Climate Leadership Group is a network of nearly 100 of the world’s largest
cities, committed to reducing greenhouse gas emissions, enhancing urban resilience, and
addressing climate impacts on a city scale.
○ Recognising that urban areas contribute a significant share of global emissions, C40
has set ambitious targets, with cities like London, New York, and Tokyo committing
to achieve net-zero emissions by 2050.
● C40 initiatives address issues specific to cities, such as transportation emissions, waste
management, and building efficiency.
○ London’s Ultra Low Emission Zone (ULEZ) has significantly reduced vehicle
emissions, and Los Angeles has set a target for 100% renewable energy by 2045.
Development Banks and Regional Financial Institutions
● Organisations like the Inter-American Development Bank (IDB) and the Asian Development
Bank (ADB) play crucial roles in financing climate projects across Latin America, Asia, and
the Pacific.
● The IDB has financed renewable energy projects in Argentina and climate-resilient
infrastructure in Central America, while the ADB has committed to providing $100 billion in
climate financing between 2019 and 2030.
● These banks not only support renewable energy but also fund adaptation projects, such as
flood control systems and drought-resistant agriculture, which are vital for regions heavily
impacted by climate change.
